{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"LOOP ITERATION 12. J8 review round 2: BLOCK, down to ONE finding — and it is a CROSS-TICKET\ninconsistency I could not easily have caught myself. J8 said the core reaches for FIFTEEN transport\nmembers; J9's description says TWENTY-ONE. Both cannot be right.\n\nRE-MEASURED rather than trusting either number or my memory:\n    call-form-only AST pattern (the NARROW one): 15\n    every attribute access on a client/transport receiver (CORRECT): 21\n    absent from the DC transport: 12\n    found ONLY by the wider pattern: create_issue, delete_issue_link, get_issue, set_parent,\n                                     set_relationship, update_issue\n\nSo J8 carried the stale undercount from my first, too-narrow audit; J9's 21 is right. Fixed, and the\nplan now explains WHY the two numbers differ — the reconciler routinely passes transport methods as\nVALUES (e.g. _call_with_retry(client.delete_issue_link, link_id)), which a call-form-only scan misses\nentirely. That explanation belongs in the ADR too: it is the reason an audit of this seam must match\nattribute ACCESS, not invocation.\n\nWorth noting what the gate did here: it cross-checked two tickets against each other. Neither ticket\nis internally inconsistent — the error was only visible by comparing them, which is a class of defect\nI had no cheap way to detect on my own."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785490268516241001,"uuid":"b95a6816-05ac-4025-85a0-98ed6b927e32"}